{{good article}}
{{Short description|Animal reliance on other individuals to raise its young}}
{{pp|small=yes}}
[[File:(Molothrus bonariensis) e ( Zonotrichia Capensis ).jpg|thumb|upright=1.3|A [[shiny cowbird]] chick (left) being fed by a [[rufous-collared sparrow]] ]]
[[File:Eastern Phoebe-nest-Brown-headed-Cowbird-egg.jpg|thumb|upright=1.3|[[Eastern phoebe]] nest with one [[brown-headed cowbird]] egg (at bottom left)]]
[[File:Fluvicola_nengeta_with_Molothrus_bonariensis.webm|thumb|Shiny cowbird parasitizing [[masked water tyrant]] in Brazil]]
'''Brood parasitism''' is a subclass of [[parasitism]] and phenomenon and behavioural pattern of  [[animal]]s that rely on others to raise their young. The strategy appears among [[bird]]s, [[insect]]s and [[fish]]. The brood parasite manipulates a [[host (biology)|host]], either of the same or of another species, to raise its young as if it were its own, usually using '''egg mimicry''', with eggs that resemble the host's. The strategy involves a form of [[aggressive mimicry]] called '''Kirbyan mimicry'''.

The [[Evolutionarily stable strategy|evolutionary strategy]] relieves the [[parasitism|parasitic]] parents from the investment of rearing young. This benefit comes at the cost of provoking an [[evolutionary arms race]] between [[Host–parasite coevolution|parasite and host as they coevolve]]: many hosts have developed strong defenses against brood parasitism, such as recognizing and ejecting parasitic eggs, or abandoning parasitized nests and starting over. It is less obvious why most hosts do care for parasite nestlings, given that for example cuckoo chicks differ markedly from host chicks in size and appearance. One explanation, the [[mafia hypothesis]], proposes that parasitic adults retaliate by destroying host nests where rejection has occurred; there is experimental evidence to support this.<ref name="Hoover 2007"/> Intraspecific brood parasitism also occurs, as in many [[duck]] species.<ref name="Yezerinac 1997"/><ref name="Andersson 1982"/>

== Evolutionary strategy ==

{{further|Mimicry#Reproductive}}

Brood parasitism is an [[Evolutionarily stable strategy |evolutionary strategy]] that relieves the [[parasite|parasitic]] parents from the investment of rearing young or building [[nest]]s for the young by getting the [[Host (biology)|host]] to raise their young for them. This enables the parasitic parents to spend more time on other activities such as foraging and producing further [[offspring]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Roldán |first1=María |last2=Soler |first2=Manuel |title=Parental-care parasitism: How do unrelated offspring attain acceptance by foster parents? |journal=Behavioral Ecology |volume=22 |issue=4 |pages=679–691 |year=2011 |doi=10.1093/beheco/arr041 |doi-access=free }}</ref>

=== Adaptations for parasitism ===

[[File:Cuckoo Eggs Mimicking Reed Warbler Eggs.JPG|thumb|Four clutches of [[reed warbler]] eggs, each containing one visibly larger [[cuckoo]] egg]]

Among specialist avian brood parasites, mimetic eggs are a nearly universal [[adaptation]]. The generalist brown-headed cowbird may have evolved an egg coloration [[mimic]]king a number of their hosts.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Peer |first1=Brian |last2=Robinson |first2=Scott |last3=Herkert |first3=James |title=Egg Rejection by Cowbird Hosts in Grasslands |journal=The Auk |volume=117 |issue=4 |pages=892–901 |doi=10.1642/0004-8038(2000)117[0892:ERBCHI]2.0.CO;2 |year=2000 |s2cid=86528269 |doi-access=free }}</ref> Size may also be important for the incubation and survival of parasitic species; it may be beneficial for parasitic eggs to be similar in size to the eggs of the host species.<ref name=Kruger2007>{{cite journal |last1=Krüger |first1=Oliver |title=Cuckoos, cowbirds and hosts: Adaptations, trade-offs and constraints |journal=Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences |volume=362 |issue=1486 |pages=1873–1886 |year=2007 |doi=10.1098/rstb.2006.1849 |pmid=17827098 |pmc=2442387}}</ref>

The eggshells of brood parasites are often thicker than those of the hosts. For example, two studies of cuckoos parasiting [[great reed warbler]]s reported thickness ratios of 1.02&nbsp;:&nbsp;0.87<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Picman |first1=Jaroslav |last2=Honza |first2=Marcel |title=How strong are eggs of the common cuckoo Cuculus canorus? |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Biology |date=21 December 2020 |volume=70 |issue=1 |doi=10.25225/jvb.20109 |no-pp=y |page=[https://bioone.org/journals/journal-of-vertebrate-biology/volume-70/issue-1/jvb.20109/How-strong-are-eggs-of-the-common-cuckoo-Cuculus-canorus/10.25225/jvb.20109.full#t01 p. 3 Table 1] |s2cid=232264070 |doi-access=free }}</ref> and 1.04&nbsp;:&nbsp;0.81.<ref name="HargitaiMoskát2010">{{cite journal |last1=Hargitai |first1=Rita |last2=Moskát |first2=Csaba |last3=Bán |first3=Miklós |last4=Gil |first4=Diego |last5=López-Rull |first5=Isabel |last6=Solymos |first6=Emese |display-authors=3 |s2cid=55241987 |title=Eggshell characteristics and yolk composition in the common cuckoo ''Cuculus canorus'': are they adapted to brood parasitism? |journal=Journal of Avian Biology|volume=41|issue=2|year=2010|pages=177–185 |doi=10.1111/j.1600-048X.2009.04818.x |bibcode=2010JAvBi..41..177H }}</ref> The function of this thick eggshell is debated. One hypothesis, the puncture resistance hypothesis, states that the thicker eggshells serve to prevent hosts from breaking the eggshell, thus killing the embryo inside. This is supported by a study in which [[marsh warbler]]s damaged their own eggs more often when attempting to break cuckoo eggs, but incurred less damage when trying to puncture great reed warbler eggs put in the nest by researchers. Another hypothesis is the laying damage hypothesis, which postulates that the eggshells are adapted to damage the eggs of the host when the former is being laid, and prevent the parasite's eggs from being damaged when the host lays its eggs.<ref name="AntonovStokke2006">{{cite journal |last1=Antonov |first1=Anton |last2=Stokke |first2=Bård G. |last3=Moksnes |first3=Arne |last4=Kleven |first4=Oddmund |last5=Honza |first5=Marcel |last6=Røskaft |first6=Eivin |title=Eggshell strength of an obligate brood parasite: a test of the puncture resistance hypothesis |journal=Behavioral Ecology and Sociobiology |volume=60 |issue=1 |year=2006 |pages=11–18 |doi=10.1007/s00265-005-0132-6 |bibcode=2006BEcoS..60...11A |s2cid=20285834}}</ref> In support of this hypothesis, eggs of the [[shiny cowbird]] parasitizing the [[Northern house wren|house wren]] and the [[chalk-browed mockingbird]] and the [[brown-headed cowbird]] parasitizing the house wren and the [[red-winged blackbird]] damaged the host's eggs when dropped, and sustained little damage when host eggs were dropped on them.<ref name="LópezFiorini2018">{{cite journal |last1=López |first1=Analía V. |last2=Fiorini |first2=Vanina D. |last3=Ellison |first3=Kevin |last4=Peer |first4=Brian D. |title=Thick eggshells of brood parasitic cowbirds protect their eggs and damage host eggs during laying |journal=Behavioral Ecology |volume=29 |issue=4 |year=2018 |pages=965–973 |doi=10.1093/beheco/ary045 |doi-access=free|hdl=11336/96586 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>

Most avian brood parasites have very short egg [[Avian incubation|incubation]] periods and rapid nestling growth. In many brood parasites, such as cuckoos and honeyguides, this short egg incubation period is due to internal incubation periods up to 24 hours longer in cuckoos than hosts. Some non-parasitic cuckoos also have longer internal incubation periods, suggesting that this longer internal incubation period was not an adaptation following brood parasitism, but predisposed birds to become brood parasites.<ref name="ccevo2012">{{cite journal |last1=Birkhead |first1=T. R. |last2=Hemmings |first2=N. |last3=Spottiswoode |first3=C. N. |last4=Mikulica |first4=O. |last5=Moskat |first5=C. |last6=Ban |first6=M. |last7=Schulze-Hagen |first7=K. |title=Internal incubation and early hatching in brood parasitic birds |journal=Proceedings of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences |volume=278 |issue=1708 |pages=1019–1024 |year=2010 |doi=10.1098/rspb.2010.1504 |pmid=20880882 |pmc=3049026 |jstor=41148724 }}</ref> This is likely facilitated by a heavier yolk in the egg providing more nutrients. Being larger than the hosts on hatching is a further adaptation to being a brood parasite.<ref name="HargitaiMoskát2010"/>

=== Evolutionary arms race ===

Bird parasites mitigate the risk of egg loss by distributing eggs amongst a number of different hosts.<ref>{{cite book |last=Attenborough |first=David |author-link=David Attenborough |title=The Life of Birds |publisher=[[Princeton University Press]] |isbn=978-0-691-01633-7 |year=1998 |page=[https://archive.org/details/lifeofbirds0000atte/page/246 246] |title-link=The Life of Birds }}</ref> As such behaviours damage the host, they often result in an [[evolutionary arms race]] between [[Host–parasite coevolution|parasite and host as they coevolve]].<ref>{{cite book |last1=Payne |first1=Robert B. |year=1997 |chapter=Avian brood parasitism |editor1-first=Dale H. |editor1-last=Clayton |editor2-first=Janice |editor2-last=Moore |title=Host-parasite evolution: General principles and avian models |pages=[https://archive.org/details/hostparasiteevol1997unse/page/338 338–369] |publisher=Oxford University Press |isbn=978-0-19-854892-8 |chapter-url=https://archive.org/details/hostparasiteevol1997unse/page/338 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Rothstein |first1=Stephen I. |title=A Model System for Coevolution: Avian Brood Parasitism |journal=Annual Review of Ecology and Systematics |volume=21 |pages=481–508 |year=1990 |doi=10.1146/annurev.ecolsys.21.1.481 |jstor=2097034}}</ref>
Some host species have strong rejection defenses, forcing the parasitic species to evolve excellent mimicry. In other species, hosts do not defend against parasites, and the parasitic mimicry is poor.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Stevens |first=Martin |author-link=Martin Stevens (biologist) |date=2013-10-21 |title=Bird brood parasitism |journal=Current Biology |volume=23 |issue=20 |pages=R909–R913 |doi=10.1016/j.cub.2013.08.025 |pmid=24156805 |doi-access=free|bibcode=2013CBio...23.R909S }}</ref>

[[File:Kirbyan mimicry.svg|thumb|upright=2|Kirbyan mimicry, in which a brood parasite mimics its host]]

Intraspecific brood parasitism among [[coot]]s significantly increases the reproductive fitness of the parasite, but only about half of the eggs laid parasitically in other coot nests survive. This implies that coots have somewhat effective anti-parasitism strategies.<ref name=lyon/> Similarly, the parasitic offspring of [[bearded reedling]]s, compared to offspring in non-parasitic nests, tend to develop much more slowly and often do not reach full maturity.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Hoi |first1=Herbert |last2=Krištofík |first2=Jan |last3=Darolová |first3=Alzbeta |title=Conspecific brood parasitism and anti-parasite strategies in relation to breeding density in female bearded tits |journal=Behaviour |volume=147 |issue=12 |pages=1533–1549 |year=2010 |doi=10.1163/000579510X511060 |jstor=20799565}}</ref>

Given that the cost to the host of egg removal by the parasite is unrecoverable, the best strategy for hosts is to avoid parasitism in the first place. This can take several forms, including selecting nest sites which are difficult to parasitize, starting [[egg incubation|incubation]] early so they are already sitting on the nests when parasites visit them early in the morning, and aggressively defending their territory.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Tucker |first1=A. M. |last2=Bulluck |first2=L. P. |year=2018 |title=No evidence for a negative effect of conspecific brood parasitism on annual survival of female Prothonotary Warblers |journal=Ibis |volume=160 |pages=447–452 |doi=10.1111/ibi.12538 |issue=2 }}</ref>

Once a parasitic egg has arrived in a host's nest, the next most optimal defense is to eject the parasitic egg. This requires the host to distinguish which eggs are not theirs, by identifying pattern differences or changes in the number of eggs.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Lyon |first1=Bruce E. |pmid=12673243 |title=Egg recognition and counting reduce costs of avian conspecific brood parasitism |journal=Nature |volume=422 |issue=6931 |pages=495–499 |year=2003 |doi=10.1038/nature01505 |bibcode=2003Natur.422..495L |s2cid=4214442 }}</ref> Eggs may be ejected by grasping, if the host has a large enough beak, or by puncturing. When the parasitic eggs are mimetic, hosts may mistake one of their own eggs for a parasite's. A host might also damage its own eggs while trying to eject a parasite's egg.<ref name="Lorenzana2001">{{cite journal |last1=Lorenzana |first1=Janice C. |last2=Sealy |first2=Spencer G. |title=Fitness costs and benefits of cowbird egg ejection by gray catbirds |journal=Behavioral Ecology |volume=12 |issue=3 |pages=325–329 |year=2001 |doi=10.1093/beheco/12.3.325|doi-access=free }}</ref> There is some evidence that nests in more shaded territories may be more vulnerable to intraspecific brood parasitism, with the shadows potentially hindering hosts' ability to recognise and reject parasitic eggs, as well as providing concealment for egg-dumping females.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Biagolini‐Jr |first=Carlos |last2=Diniz |first2=Pedro |last3=Macedo |first3=Regina H. |last4=Webster |first4=Michael S. |date=2025 |title=Shading by vegetation facilitates cryptic reproductive behaviour in a tropical songbird |url=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ibi.13363 |journal=Ibis |language=en |volume=167 |issue=1 |pages=277–285 |doi=10.1111/ibi.13363 |issn=0019-1019|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

Among hosts that do not eject parasitic eggs, some abandon parasitized nests and start over again. However, at high enough parasitism frequencies, this becomes [[maladaptation|maladaptive]] as the new nest will most likely also be parasitized. Some host species modify their nests to exclude the parasitic egg, either by weaving over the egg or by rebuilding a new nest over the existing one. For instance, [[American coot]]s may kick the parasites' eggs out, or build a new nest beside the brood nests where the parasites' chicks starve to death.<ref name=lyon>{{cite journal |doi=10.1006/anbe.1993.1273 |title=Conspecific brood parasitism as a flexible female reproductive tactic in American coots |journal=Animal Behaviour |volume=46 |issue=5 |pages=911–928 |year=1993 |last1=Lyon |first1=Bruce E. |bibcode=1993AnBeh..46..911L |s2cid=53188986 }}</ref> In the [[western Bonelli's warbler]], a small host, small dummy parasitic eggs were always ejected, whilst with large dummy parasitic eggs, nest desertion was more frequent.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Roncalli |first1=Gianluca |last2=Ibáñez-Álamo |first2=Juan Diego |last3=Soler |first3=Manuel |title=Size and material of model parasitic eggs affect the rejection response of Western Bonelli's Warbler ''Phylloscopus bonelli'' |journal=Ibis |volume=159 |issue=1 |pages=113–123 |year=2017 |doi=10.1111/ibi.12431|url=https://pure.rug.nl/ws/files/99796695/Roncalli_et_al_2017_Ibis.pdf }}</ref>

=== Mafia hypothesis ===

{{main|Mafia hypothesis}}

There is a question as to why the majority of the hosts of brood parasites care for the nestlings of their parasites. Not only do these brood parasites usually differ significantly in size and appearance, but it is also highly probable that they reduce the [[reproductive success]] of their hosts. The "mafia hypothesis" proposes that when a brood parasite discovers that its egg has been rejected, it destroys the host's nest and injures or kills the nestlings. The threat of such a response may encourage compliant behavior from the host.<ref name="Soler Soler Martinez 1995">{{cite journal |last1=Soler |first1=M. |last2=Soler |first2=J. J. |last3=Martinez |first3=J. G. |last4=Moller |first4=A. P.|title=Magpie Host Manipulation by Great Spotted Cuckoos: Evidence for an Avian Mafia? |journal=Evolution |volume=49 |issue=4 |pages=770–775 |year=1995 |doi=10.2307/2410329 |pmid=28565143 |jstor=2410329}}</ref> Mafia-like behavior occurs in the [[brown-headed cowbird]] of North America, and the [[great spotted cuckoo]] of Europe. The great spotted cuckoo lays most of its eggs in the nests of the [[European magpie]]. It repeatedly visits nests it has parasitised, a precondition for the mafia hypothesis. In experiments, nests from which the parasite's egg has been removed are destroyed by the cuckoo, supporting the hypothesis. An alternative explanation is that the destruction encourages the magpie host to build a new nest, giving the cuckoo another opportunity for parasitism.<ref name="Soler Soler Martinez 1995"/> Similarly, the brown-headed cowbird parasitises the [[prothonotary warbler]]. In other experiments, 56% of egg-ejected nests were predated upon, against 6% of non-ejected nests. 85% of parasitized nests rebuilt by hosts were destroyed. Hosts that ejected parasite eggs produced 60% fewer young than those that accepted the cowbird eggs.<ref name="Hoover 2007">{{cite journal |last1=Hoover |first1=Jeffrey P. |last2=Robinson |first2=Scott K. |title=Retaliatory mafia behavior by a parasitic cowbird favors host acceptance of parasitic eggs |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences |volume=104 |issue=11 |pages=4479–4483 |year=2007 |doi=10.1073/pnas.0609710104 |pmid=17360549 |pmc=1838626 |jstor=25426858 |bibcode=2007PNAS..104.4479H |doi-access=free }}</ref>

=== Similarity hypothesis ===

[[Common cuckoo]] females have been proposed to select hosts with similar egg characteristics to her own. The hypothesis suggests that the female monitors a population of potential hosts and chooses nests from within this group.<ref name=Moksnes>{{cite journal |last1=Moksnes |first1=Arne |last2=Øskaft |first2=Eivin R. |title=Egg-morphs and host preference in the common cuckoo (Cuculus canorus): An analysis of cuckoo and host eggs from European museum collections |journal=Journal of Zoology |volume=236 |issue=4 |pages=625–648 |year=1995 |doi=10.1111/j.1469-7998.1995.tb02736.x}}</ref> Study of museum nest collections shows a similarity between cuckoo eggs and typical eggs of the host species. A low percentage of parasitized nests were shown to contain cuckoo eggs not corresponding to the specific host egg morph. In these mismatched nests a high percent of the cuckoo eggs were shown to correlate to the egg morph of another host species with similar nesting sites. This has been pointed to as evidence for selection by similarity.<ref name=Moksnes/> The hypothesis has been criticised for providing no mechanism for choosing nests, nor identifying cues by which they might be recognised.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Vogl |first1=Wolfgang |last2=Taborsky |first2=Michael |last3=Taborsky |first3=Barbara |last4=Teuschl |first4=Yvonne |last5=Honza |first5=Marcel |s2cid=17818119 |title=Cuckoo females preferentially use specific habitats when searching for host nests |journal=Animal Behaviour |volume=64 |issue=6 |pages=843–850 |year=2002 |doi=10.1006/anbe.2003.1967 |bibcode=2002AnBeh..64..843V }}</ref>

=== Hosts raise offspring ===

Sometimes hosts are completely unaware that they are caring for a bird that is not their own. This most commonly occurs because the host cannot differentiate the parasitic eggs from their own. It may also occur when hosts temporarily leave the nest after laying the eggs. The parasites lay their own eggs into these nests so their nestlings share the food provided by the host. It may occur in other situations. For example, female [[eider]]s prefer to lay eggs in the nests with one or two existing eggs of others because the first egg is the most vulnerable to predators. The presence of others' eggs reduces the probability that a predator will attack her egg when a female leaves the nest after laying the first egg.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Robertson |first1=Gregory J. |title=Egg adoption can explain joint egg-laying in common eiders |journal=Behavioral Ecology and Sociobiology |volume=43 |issue=4–5 |pages=289–296 |year=1998 |doi=10.1007/s002650050493 |jstor=4601519 |bibcode=1998BEcoS..43..289R |s2cid=6187623 }}</ref>

Sometimes, the parasitic offspring kills the host nest-mates during competition for resources. For example, parasitic [[cowbird]] chicks kill the host nest-mates if food intake for each of them is low, but not if the food intake is adequate.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Gloag |first1=Ros |last2=Tuero |first2=Diego T. |last3=Fiorini |first3=Vanina D. |last4=Reboreda |first4=Juan C. |last5=Kacelnik |first5=Alex |s2cid=3945653 |title=The economics of nestmate killing in avian brood parasites: A provisions trade-off |journal=Behavioral Ecology |volume=23 |issue=1 |pages=132–140 |year=2012 |doi=10.1093/beheco/arr166 |doi-access=free |hdl=11336/68228 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>

== Taxonomic range ==

=== Birds ===

{{further|List of brood parasitic passerines}}

==== Intraspecific ====

[[File:Sask duck.jpg|thumb|The [[common goldeneye|goldeneye]] often lays its eggs in the nests of other females, one of 74 species of [[Anseriformes]] to do so.<ref name="Yom-Toy 2001"/>]]

In many [[Monogamy in animals#Social monogamy|socially monogamous]] bird species, there are [[extra-pair mating]]s resulting in males outside the pair bond siring offspring and used by males to escape from the [[parental investment]] in raising their offspring.<ref name="Yezerinac 1997">{{cite journal |last1=Yezerinac |first1=Stephen M. |last2=Weatherhead |first2=Patrick J. |year=1997 |title=Extra-Pair Mating, Male Plumage Coloration and Sexual Selection in yellow warblers (''Dendroica petechia'') |journal=Proceedings of the Royal Society B |volume=264 |issue=1381 |pages=527–532 |doi=10.1098/rspb.1997.0075 |pmc=1688387 |bibcode=1997RSPSB.264..527Y }}</ref> In [[duck]] species such as the [[Common goldeneye|goldeneye]], this form of [[cuckold]]ry is taken a step further, as females often lay their eggs in the nests of other individuals.<ref name="Andersson 1982">{{cite journal |last1=Andersson |first1=Malte |last2=Eriksson |first2=Mats O. G. |title=Nest Parasitism in Goldeneyes ''Bucephala clangula'': Some Evolutionary Aspects |journal=The American Naturalist |volume=120 |issue=1 |pages=1–16 |year=1982 |doi=10.1086/283965 |jstor=2461081 |bibcode=1982ANat..120....1A |s2cid=86699716 }}</ref> Intraspecific brood parasitism has been recorded in 234 bird species, including 74 [[anseriformes]], 66 [[passeriformes]], 32 [[galliformes]], 19 [[charadriiformes]], 8 [[gruiformes]], 6 [[podicipediformes]], and small numbers of species in other orders.<ref name="Yom-Toy 2001">{{cite journal |last1=Yom-Tov |first1=Yoram |title=An updated list and some comments on the occurrence of intraspecific nest parasitism in birds |journal=Ibis |date=2001 |volume=143 |issue=1 |pages=133–143 |doi=10.1111/j.1474-919X.2001.tb04177.x}}</ref>

=== Quasiparasitism ===

Quasiparasitism  is a subtype of intraspecific brood parasitism in which the female parent escapes parental investment, but the offspring is still raised by its biological father. A female mates with a male, then deposits the egg in the nest of another female that is attended by that same male. This behavior was first hypothesized by [[Marion Petrie]] during her work with common moorhens, while the term quasi-parasitism was coined by Peter Wrege and Stephen Emlen to describe behavior they observed in white-fronted bee-eaters; however, since confirming quasiparasitism requires both extensive behavioral observations and genetic data, it is often difficult to confirm whether a given offspring is the result of quasiparasitic behavior.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Griffith |first=Simon C. |last2=Lyon |first2=Bruce E. |last3=Montgomerie |first3=Robert |date=2004-07-01 |title=Quasi-parasitism in birds |url=https://doi.org/10.1007/s00265-004-0766-9 |journal=Behavioral Ecology and Sociobiology |volume=56 |issue=3 |pages=191–200 |doi=10.1007/s00265-004-0766-9 |issn=1432-0762|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Wrege |first=Peter H. |last2=Emlen |first2=Stephen T. |date=1987-03-01 |title=Biochemical determination of parental uncertainty in white-fronted bee-eaters |url=https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00299728 |journal=Behavioral Ecology and Sociobiology |volume=20 |issue=3 |pages=153–160 |doi=10.1007/BF00299728 |issn=1432-0762|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

==== Interspecific ====

[[File:Reed warbler cuckoo.jpg|upright|thumb|left|A [[Eurasian reed warbler]] raising a [[common cuckoo]] ]]

Interspecific brood-parasites include the [[indigobird]]s, [[Viduidae|whydahs]], and [[honeyguide]]s in [[Africa]], [[cowbird]]s, [[Old World]] [[cuckoo]]s, [[black-headed duck]]s, and some [[Cuckoos#Brood parasitism|New World cuckoos]] in the Americas. Seven independent origins of obligate interspecific brood parasitism in birds have been proposed. While there is still some controversy over when and how many origins of interspecific brood parasitism have occurred, recent phylogenetic analyses suggest two origins in Passeriformes (once in New World cowbirds: Icteridae, and once in African Finches: Viduidae); three origins in Old World and New World cuckoos (once in Cuculinae, Phaenicophaeinae, and in Neomorphinae-Crotophaginae); a single origin in Old World honeyguides (Indicatoridae); and in a single species of waterfowl, the black-headed duck (''Heteronetta atricapilla'').<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Aragon |last2=Moller |last3=Soler |last4=Soler |s2cid=16923328 |title=Molecular phylogeny of cuckoos supports a polyphyletic origin of brood parasitism |journal=Journal of Evolutionary Biology |volume=12 |issue=3 |pages=495–506 |year=1999 |doi=10.1046/j.1420-9101.1999.00052.x|doi-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Sorenson |first1=Michael D. |last2=Payne |first2=Robert B. |title=A Single Ancient Origin of Brood Parasitism in African Finches: Implications for Host-Parasite Coevolution |journal=Evolution |volume=55 |issue=12 |pages=2550–2567 |year=2001 |doi=10.1554/0014-3820(2001)055[2550:asaoob]2.0.co;2 |pmid=11831669 |url=https://deepblue.lib.umich.edu/bitstream/2027.42/72018/1/j.0014-3820.2001.tb00768.x.pdf |hdl=2027.42/72018 |s2cid=198154320 |hdl-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Sorenson |first1=Michael D. |last2=Payne |first2=Robert B. |title=Molecular Genetic Perspectives on Avian Brood Parasitism |journal=Integrative and Comparative Biology |volume=42 |issue=2 |pages=388–400 |year=2002 |doi=10.1093/icb/42.2.388 |pmid=21708732 |doi-access=free }}</ref>

Most avian brood parasites are [[specialist (biology)|specialists]] which parasitize only a single host species or a small group of closely related host species, but four out of the five parasitic cowbirds (all except the [[screaming cowbird]]) are generalists which parasitize a wide variety of hosts; the [[brown-headed cowbird]] has 221 known hosts. They usually lay only one egg per nest, although in some cases, particularly the [[cowbird]]s, several females may use the same host nest.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Strausberger |first1=Bill |title=Temporal patterns of host availability, brown-headed cowbird brood parasitism, and parasite egg mass |journal=Oecologia |date=August 1988 |volume=116 |issue=1–2 |pages=267–274 |doi=10.1007/s004420050588 |pmid=28308536 |s2cid=8732061 |url=https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s004420050588.pdf }}</ref>

[[File:Asian Koel Being Fed by Black-collared Starling (cropped).jpg|thumb|A [[black-collared starling]] feeding an [[Asian koel]] ]]

The [[common cuckoo]] presents an interesting case in which the [[species]] as a whole parasitizes a wide variety of hosts, including the [[reed warbler]] and [[dunnock]], but individual females specialize in a single species. [[Gene]]s regulating egg [[animal coloration|coloration]] appear to be passed down exclusively along the [[maternal]] line, allowing females to lay mimetic eggs in the nest of the species they specialize in. Females generally parasitize nests of the species which raised them. Male common cuckoos [[fertilization|fertilize]] females of all lines, which maintains sufficient [[gene flow]] among the different maternal lines to prevent [[speciation]].<!--comment not a conscious choice--><ref name=vogl>{{cite journal |last1=Vogl |first1=Wolfgang |last2=Taborsky |first2=Michael |last3=Taborsky |first3=Barbara |last4=Teuschl |first4=Yvonne |last5=Honza |first5=Marcel |s2cid=17818119 |title=Cuckoo females preferentially use specific habitats when searching for host nests |journal=Animal Behaviour |volume=64 |issue=6 |pages=843–850 |year=2002 |doi=10.1006/anbe.2003.1967 |bibcode=2002AnBeh..64..843V }}</ref>

The mechanisms of host selection by female cuckoos are somewhat unclear, though several hypotheses have been suggested in attempt to explain the choice. These include genetic [[Biological inheritance|inheritance]] of host preference, host imprinting on young birds, returning to place of birth and subsequently choosing a host randomly ("natal philopatry"), choice based on preferred nest site (nest-site hypothesis), and choice based on preferred [[habitat]] ([[habitat-selection hypothesis]]). Of these hypotheses the nest-site selection and habitat selection have been most supported by experimental analysis.<ref name=vogl/><ref name=teuschl>{{cite journal |last1=Teuschl |first1=Yvonne |last2=Taborsky |first2=Barbara |last3=Taborsky |first3=Michael |s2cid=25931055 |title=How do cuckoos find their hosts? The role of habitat imprinting |journal=Animal Behaviour |year=1998 |volume=56 |issue=6 |pages=1425–1433 |doi=10.1006/anbe.1998.0931 |pmid=9933539 |bibcode=1998AnBeh..56.1425T }}</ref>

=== Fish ===

==== Mouthbrooding parasites ====
[[File:Synodontis multipunctatus J. Green.jpg|thumb|The catfish ''[[Synodontis multipunctatus]]'' is a brood parasite of several [[mouthbrooding]] [[cichlid]] fish.]]

A [[mochokid]] catfish of [[Lake Tanganyika]], ''[[Synodontis multipunctatus]]'', is a brood parasite of several [[mouthbrooding]] [[cichlid]] fish. The catfish eggs are incubated in the host's mouth, and—in the manner of cuckoos—hatch before the host's own eggs. The young catfish eat the host fry inside the host's mouth, effectively taking up virtually the whole of the host's parental investment.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Sato |first1=Tetsu |title=A brood parasitic catfish of mouthbrooding cichlid fishes in Lake Tanganyika |journal=Nature |volume=323 |issue=6083 |pages=58–59 |year=1986 |doi=10.1038/323058a0 |pmid=3748180|bibcode=1986Natur.323...58S |s2cid=4330270 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Blažek |first1=Radim |last2=Polačik |first2=Matej |last3=Smith |first3=Carl |last4=Honza |first4=Marcel |last5=Meyer |first5=Axel |last6=Reichard |first6=Martin|title=Success of cuckoo catfish brood parasitism reflects coevolutionary history and individual experience of their cichlid hosts |journal=Science Advances |volume=4 |issue=5 |article-number=eaar4380 |year=2018 |doi=10.1126/sciadv.aar4380  |doi-access=free|pmid=29732407 |pmc=5931752|bibcode=2018SciA....4.4380B }}</ref>

==== Nest parasites ====

A [[cyprinid]] minnow, ''[[Pungtungia herzi]]'' is a brood parasite of the [[Percichthyidae|percichthyid]] freshwater perch ''[[Siniperca kawamebari]]'', which live in the south of the Japanese islands of [[Honshu]], [[Kyushu]] and [[Shikoku]], and in [[South Korea]]. Host males guard territories against intruders during the breeding season, creating a patch of reeds as a spawning site or "nest". Females (one or more per site) visit the site to lay eggs, which the male then defends. The parasite's eggs are smaller and stickier than the host's. 65.5% of host sites were parasitised in a study area.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Baba |first1=Reiko |last2=Nagata |first2=Yoshikazu |last3=Yamagishi |first3=Satoshi |doi=10.1016/s0003-3472(05)80707-9 |title=Brood parasitism and egg robbing among three freshwater fish |journal=Animal Behaviour |volume=40 |issue=4 |pages=776–778 |year=1990 |bibcode=1990AnBeh..40..776B |s2cid=54373427 }}</ref>

=== Insects ===

==== Kleptoparasites ====
[[File:Cuckoo bee.jpg|thumb|A [[cuckoo bee]] from the genus ''[[Nomada]]'']]
{{Main|Kleptoparasitism}}

There are many different types of [[cuckoo bee]]s, all of which lay their eggs in the nest cells of other bees, but they are normally described as [[kleptoparasite]]s (Greek: klepto-, to steal), rather than as brood parasites, because the immature stages are almost never fed directly by the adult hosts. Instead, they simply take food gathered by their hosts. Examples of [[cuckoo bee]]s are ''[[Coelioxys rufitarsis]]'', ''[[Melecta separata]]'', ''[[Nomada]]'' and ''[[Epeoloides]]''.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Pawelek |first1=Jaime |last2=Coville |first2=Rollin |title=Cuckoo Bees |url=http://www.helpabee.org/cuckoo-bees.html |publisher=University of California Berkeley |access-date=24 February 2015}}</ref>

Kleptoparasitism in insects is not restricted to bees; several lineages of [[wasp]] including most of the Chrysididae, the [[cuckoo wasp]]s, are kleptoparasites. The cuckoo wasps lay their eggs in the nests of other wasps, such as those of the [[Potter wasp|potters]] and [[mud dauber]]s.<ref>{{cite web |title=Cuckoo Wasps |url=http://museum.wa.gov.au/research/collections/terrestrial-zoology/entomology-insect-collection/entomology-factsheets/cuckoo-wasps |publisher=Western Australian Museum |access-date=24 February 2015}}</ref> Some species of beetle are kleptoparasites, as well. ''[[Meloe americanus]]'' larvae are known to enter bee nests and feed on the provisions reserved for the bee larva.<ref name="Bionomics and Taxonomy of Meloe">{{cite web |last1=Pinto |first1=John |title=Bionomics and Taxonomy of Meloe (Coleoptera, Meloidae), With a Classification of the New World Species |url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/302291519 |website=ProQuest |id={{ProQuest|302291519}} }}</ref>

==== True brood parasites ====
{{Further|Nest usurpation|Myrmecophily}}

True brood parasitism is rare among insects. Cuckoo bumblebees (the subgenus ''[[Psithyrus]]'') are among the few insects which, like cuckoos and cowbirds, are fed by adult hosts. Their queens kill and replace the existing queen of a colony of the host species, and then use the host workers to feed their brood.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kawakita |first1=Atsushi |last2=Sota |first2=Teiji |last3=Ito |first3=Masao |last4=Ascher |first4=John S. |last5=Tanaka |first5=Hiroyuki |last6=Kato |first6=Makoto |last7=Roubik |first7=David W.|title=Phylogeny, historical biogeography, and character evolution in bumble bees (Bombus: Apidae) based on simultaneous analysis of three nuclear gene sequences |journal=Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution |volume=31 |issue=2 |pages=799–804 |year=2004 |doi=10.1016/j.ympev.2003.12.003 |pmid=15062814 |bibcode=2004MolPE..31..799K }}</ref>

[[File:Yellow Paper Wasp.jpg|thumb|Nest of ''[[Polistes dominula]]'', host to the [[cuckoo wasp]] ''[[Polistes semenowi|P.&nbsp;semenowi]]''{{efn|name=semenowiVSsulcifer}}]]

One of only four true brood-parasitic wasps is ''[[Polistes semenowi]]''.{{efn|name=semenowiVSsulcifer|''Polistes semenowi'' was mistakenly named ''[[Polistes sulcifer]]'' until 2017. This note has been included for continuity relative to pre-2017 citations used in this article.}} This paper wasp has lost the ability to build its own nest, and relies on its host, ''[[Polistes dominula|P. dominula]]'', to raise its brood. The adult host feeds the parasite larvae directly, unlike typical kleptoparasitic insects.<ref name="Dapporto">{{cite journal |last1=Dapporto |first1=L. |last2=Cervo |first2=R |last3=Sledge |first3=M. F. |last4=Turillazzi |first4=S. |title=Rank integration in dominance hierarchies of host colonies by the paper wasp social parasite Polistes sulcifer (Hymenoptera, Vespidae) |journal=Journal of Insect Physiology |volume=50 |issue=2–3 |pages=217–223 |year=2004 |doi=10.1016/j.jinsphys.2003.11.012 |pmid=15019524 |bibcode=2004JInsP..50..217D }}</ref><ref name= "Ortolani">{{cite journal |last1=Ortolani |first1=Irene |last2=Cervo |first2=Rita |title=Coevolution of daily activity timing in a host-parasite system |journal=Biological Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=96 |issue=2 |pages=399–405 |year=2009 |doi=10.1111/j.1095-8312.2008.01139.x |doi-access=free }}</ref> Such insect social parasites are often closely related to their hosts, an observation known as [[Emery's rule]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Deslippe |first=Richard |title=Social Parasitism in Ants |url=http://www.nature.com/scitable/knowledge/library/social-parasitism-in-ants-13256421 |publisher=Nature Education Knowledge |year=2010 |access-date=23 June 2022}}</ref>

Host insects are sometimes tricked into bringing offspring of another species into their own nests, as with the parasitic butterfly, ''[[Phengaris rebeli]]'', and the host ant ''[[Myrmica schencki]]''.<ref name="AkinoKnapp1999">{{cite journal |last1=Akino |first1=T. |last2=Knapp |first2=J. J. |last3=Thomas |first3=J. A. |last4=Elmes |first4=G. W. |title=Chemical mimicry and host specificity in the butterfly Maculinea rebeli, a social parasite of Myrmica ant colonies |journal=Proceedings of the Royal Society B |volume=266 |issue=1427 |pages=1419–1426 |year=1999 |doi=10.1098/rspb.1999.0796 |jstor=51672 |pmc=1690087 }}</ref> The butterfly larvae release chemicals that confuse the host ant into believing that the ''P.&nbsp;rebeli'' larvae are actually ant larvae.<ref name="AkinoKnapp1999"/> Thus, the ''M.&nbsp;schencki'' ants bring back the ''P.&nbsp;rebeli'' larvae to their nests and feed them, much like the chicks of cuckoos and other brood-parasitic birds. This is also the case for the parasitic butterfly, ''[[Niphanda fusca]]'', and its host ant ''[[Camponotus japonicus]]''. The butterfly releases [[cuticular hydrocarbons]] that mimic those of the host male ant. The ant then brings the third instar larvae back into its own nest and raises them until pupation.<ref name="HojoWada-Katsumata2009">{{cite journal |last1=Hojo |first1=Masaru K. |last2=Wada-Katsumata |first2=Ayako |last3=Akino |first3=Toshiharu |last4=Yamaguchi |first4=Susumu |last5=Ozaki |first5=Mamiko |last6=Yamaoka |first6=Ryohei |title=Chemical disguise as particular caste of host ants in the ant inquiline parasite Niphanda fusca (Lepidoptera: Lycaenidae) |journal=Proceedings of the Royal Society B |volume=276 |issue=1656 |pages=551–558 |year=2009 |doi=10.1098/rspb.2008.1064 |pmid=18842547 |pmc=2664337 |bibcode=2009PBioS.276..551H }}</ref>

